.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-19lr1c_implement-private-atomic-relay-install-and-reuse/README.md:10:1. Only a temporary file that passed exact size and SHA-256 verification is chmodded and atomically renamed into a validated Relux-owned versioned regular-file path. 2. Existing assets are reused only after owner, type, mode, size, and checksum validation; a known mismatch is never executed or silently accepted. 3. Concurrent bootstrap attempts converge on one valid final file or a stable typed conflict without partial publication, unrelated deletion, symlink following, or cross-device non-atomic rename. 4. Read-only home, unsafe cache, unavailable safe temporary directory, noexec, chmod or rename failure, cancellation, and SSH loss leave no published invalid asset and map to specific capability reasons. 5. Controlled-host tests cover first install, reuse, upgrade, competing versions, symlink attacks, stale files, permission failures, noexec, interruption at every operation, and Relux-owned cleanup only.
.task-board/EPIC-260716-3fyjn0_manual-validation-and-approvals/STORY-260716-2e86nb_m3-physical-validation/TASK-260715-2wnw59_run-mac-ipv6-sleep-captive-matrix/README.md:7:In scope: named Apple-silicon Mac and supported macOS or toolchain; Wi-Fi and Ethernet where available; IPv4, IPv6, dual-stack, and controlled NAT64 or IPv6-only fixture when available; compatible and fail-closed modes; full and degraded capability; sleep and wake; captive fixture; host-app kill; cancel during start; stop reasons; repeated loops; TCP, DNS, UDP, and QUIC probes; authorized captures; memory, energy, routes, counters, cleanup. Out of scope: unsupported Intel claims, unauthorized public networks, production traffic, forcing unavailable cellular behavior, absolute kill-switch claims, or waiving red rows.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-1iwpn0_document-bootstrap-capability-reasons-and-operations/README.md:1:# Document bootstrap capability reasons and operator procedures
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-1iwpn0_document-bootstrap-capability-reasons-and-operations/README.md:7:In scope: authenticated-SSH precondition; platform mapping; asset and manifest selection; fixed command families; cache and temporary path policy; upload backpressure; size and hash verification; safe readback fallback; install and reuse; stdio launch; handshake and health; failure and reason table; cleanup; privacy; test commands; troubleshooting. Out of scope: credentials, SFTP guidance, root or system service installation, UDP association internals, user interface copy, M3 network reconnect, standalone relay distribution, and changing implementation behavior during documentation.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-1jga46_implement-shell-safe-relay-path-and-command-builder/README.md:4:Implement typed construction of the fixed remote bootstrap operations and private user-owned paths, quoting every dynamic token and rejecting unsafe directory, file, or command state rather than exposing a general shell API.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-1jga46_implement-shell-safe-relay-path-and-command-builder/README.md:10:1. Callers choose only typed bootstrap operations and validated path components; no API accepts an arbitrary command string or unbounded remote output. 2. Every dynamic token round-trips as one shell argument across spaces, quotes, metacharacters, newlines, leading dashes, and Unicode policy cases or is rejected before channel open. 3. The cache path and temporary fallback are derived from fixed remote user context, created with private permissions, and rejected on unsafe owner, symlink, non-directory, traversal, control character, or length state. 4. Rendered commands never contain profile secrets, private-key material, passphrases, destination traffic, raw manifest content, or remotely returned diagnostic text. 5. Golden and hostile-input tests execute rendered plans in a controlled shell fixture and prove exact argv, no injected side effect, stable error mapping, and cleanup commands for every partial state.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3kga9i_document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics/README.md:4:Publish the technical and user-copy contract for full, degraded, failed, and relay-restoration behavior, including traffic tables, reason codes, DNS safety, UDP and QUIC limitations, snapshots, recovery, test evidence, privacy, and handoffs to M3 and product UX.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3kga9i_document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics/README.md:7:In scope: mode and transition diagrams; traffic matrix for TCP, DNS, general UDP, and system exceptions; readiness predicates; finite reason catalog; relay build, feature, limit, and health summaries; safe-DNS policy; UDP rejection; retry and restoration; failed behavior; provider versus system session state; diagnostics and support redaction; automated, harness, iPhone, and Mac evidence; M3 and M4 ownership. Out of scope: final visual design, marketing claims, absolute kill-switch claims, M3 path reconnect procedures, includeAllNetworks implementation, privacy policy approval, analytics, and changing behavior while documenting it.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/TASK-260715-3kga9i_document-capability-modes-limitations-and-diagnostics/README.md:10:1. Mode and transition diagrams match the implemented state contract and show exact readiness, cleanup, retry, full restoration, and failed boundaries. 2. A traffic table states what happens to new and existing TCP, UDP, DNS, and relevant QUIC-family traffic in every state and accurately discloses Apple system exceptions without a cryptographically absolute kill-switch claim. 3. The finite reason catalog maps each cause to mode, user-safe summary, operator diagnostics, retry eligibility, and remediation without destinations, queries, payloads, credentials, or remote-controlled strings. 4. Reproduction sections link state, fault, leak, harness, iPhone, and Mac evidence and list expected snapshots, captures, memory, counters, and cleanup results. 5. Concrete handoffs identify M3 lane, path, NAT64, sleep, captive, fail-closed, and QUIC work plus M4 status UI and privacy copy, and the document does not claim those later behaviors exist.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2qzczm_resilience-and-performance/STORY-260715-19ii11_performance-and-resource-tuning/TASK-260715-1ok93q_build-reproducible-resilience-benchmark-harness/README.md:7:In scope: local or controlled remote TCP, DNS, UDP, QUIC, and SSH fixtures; short, bulk, idle, burst, mixed, upload, download, and bidirectional traffic; byte hashes; 100, 250, 500, and configuration-limit stages; MTUs; one, two, and four lanes; window profiles; deterministic clock and seeds where possible; network condition adapter; receiver stall and bounded buffers; failure triggers; start and stop loops; metrics export; authorized capture hooks; cleanup checks. Out of scope: production-user traffic, unbounded public load, device-specific UI automation beyond harness triggers, physical-only OS behavior claims, changing product code to fit the harness, or automatic parameter optimization.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2etfkl_relay-bootstrap-and-session/TASK-260715-1jga46_implement-shell-safe-relay-path-and-command-builder/progress.md:22:- [ ] Expose typed fixed bootstrap operations with no arbitrary command escape hatch
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/README.md:4:Expose truthful full, degraded, and failed tunnel capabilities and preserve useful TCP plus tunnel-owned safe DNS when the relay cannot be installed, negotiated, or kept alive. Reject unsupported UDP explicitly, prevent ordinary DNS fallback, and restore full mode only after a clean relay handshake.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/README.md:7:In scope: capability bit and reason taxonomy; full, degraded, failed, and reasserting-compatible state contract; relay build and limit evidence; versioned provider snapshots; bootstrap, handshake, process, and lane failure mapping; association invalidation; deterministic degraded safe-DNS policy using the existing SSH DNS-over-TCP path or an explicitly approved tunneled DoH alternative; TCP continuity; explicit UDP and QUIC-family rejection policy input; fail-safe DNS behavior; bounded reprobe and full-mode restoration; diagnostics, user and operator documentation; state, fault, leak, harness, and physical transition tests. Out of scope: implementing TCP direct forwarding, M3 multi-lane scheduling, general path-change reconnect, includeAllNetworks fail-closed mode, captive or NAT64 transitions, final UI visuals, analytics, and silent physical fallback.
.task-board/EPIC-260715-2lz67t_udp-relay-and-degraded-mode/STORY-260715-2ungml_capability-and-degraded-mode/README.md:10:1. A versioned capability model distinguishes usable full mode, usable degraded TCP plus safe DNS, mandatory failure, and reasserting-compatible transitions with stable privacy-safe reason codes. 2. Unsupported platform, absent asset, bootstrap failure, checksum or version mismatch, relay exit, framing failure, and relay-lane loss invalidate UDP associations and enter degraded mode only when TCP and the selected safe-DNS path are actually usable. 3. In degraded mode TCP remains usable, DNS uses only the approved tunneled fallback, ordinary UDP is rejected explicitly, and controlled access-side monitoring observes zero ordinary DNS or application UDP fallback to the physical interface. 4. Full mode is advertised again only after a fresh validated relay handshake and limits exchange; stale callbacks or sessions cannot resurrect capability. 5. Automated and physical transition evidence covers startup degraded, full-to-degraded, degraded-to-full, safe-DNS failure, stop, repeated reprobe, bounded memory, truthful snapshots, redacted diagnostics, and user-facing limitations.
