## Accepted facts safe to carry forward

1. ADR-005 and ADR-006 in `.spec/decisions.md` require a rootless relay over a
   long-lived SSH exec/stdin/stdout channel and exec-stdin upload, with no SFTP.
2. `.spec/relay-protocol.md` is the current protocol-v1 wire source. All integers
   are unsigned, big-endian/network order. It defines the hello, envelope,
   message values, HEV UDP-in-TCP payload family, association ownership, close
   behavior, and privacy constraints.
3. `TASK-260715-3bdplx` is accepted: the relay implementation language is Go
   1.26.5, standard-library only, `CGO_ENABLED=0`, module
   `github.com/relux-works/relux-tunnel/relay` rooted at `relay/`. No third-party
   Go runtime module, cgo, or `unsafe` use is permitted without a new review.
4. `TASK-260715-2nfz7w` is accepted: shared Apple-side contracts live in
   `ReluxTunnelCore`; iOS/macOS adapter modules remain thin and do not own relay
   framing.
5. `TASK-260720-100wu6` is accepted: transport-neutral relay I/O consumes
   `SSHTransport.openExecChannel(request:policy:)` and the returned
   `SSHExecChannel`/`SSHByteChannel` surface: bounded `read(maximumBytes:)`,
   partial `writeSome`, `finishWriting`, stderr draining, exit observation,
   receive-window snapshots, cancellation, reset, and idempotent close.
6. `TASK-260715-18owh7` remains the sole binding decision for association count,
   queued bytes, datagram size, idle timeout, hard ceilings, and whether v1
   exchanges or only locally caps each value. This binding task must not invent
   those numeric values or wire fields.

## Current spec-derived framing contract (fact, not a new decision)

### Session establishment

- Client hello is exactly 12 bytes:
  `magic[4]="RLXR" | version:u16 | flags:u16 | maxFrame:u32`.
- Server hello is exactly 16 bytes:
  `magic[4]="RLXR" | version:u16 | status:u16 | features:u32 | maxFrame:u32`.
- Magic, version, status, flags, and `maxFrame` are validated before any
  envelope-sized allocation. No downgrade guessing is allowed.
- The exact broader resource-limit exchange is deferred to
  `TASK-260715-18owh7`; `TASK-260715-2azda7` and
  `TASK-260715-1y1g1u` remain blocked by that decision.

### Multiplexed message stream

- Every message is `frameLength:u32 | type:u8 | flags:u8 |
  associationID:u32 | payload[frameLength-6]`.
- The four-byte length prefix is not included in `frameLength`; the minimum
  body is 6 bytes, so the minimum on-stream message is 10 bytes.
- `frameLength` must be validated against 6, the negotiated maximum frame, the
  local hard cap, and integer-overflow rules before allocating or waiting for a
  body.
- Reserved flags must be zero. Unknown v1 type, invalid length, illegal
  direction, or unrecoverable framing state closes the session. A malformed
  datagram may close only its association when the frame is safely attributable.
- A decoder retains at most one fixed prefix plus the configured bounded body.
  It must accept every split boundary and multiple coalesced frames without
  assuming SSH-read boundaries equal protocol-frame boundaries.

### UDP datagram payload and socket-use gate

- `UDP_DATAGRAM` payload is
  `MSGLEN:u16 | HDRLEN:u8 | ATYP:u8 | DST.ADDR:variable |
  DST.PORT:u16 | DATA:variable`, using upstream HEV UDP-in-TCP semantics.
- `ATYP` supports IPv4, IPv6, and domain. Outer length and inner `MSGLEN`/
  `HDRLEN` are independently validated.
- The client allocates nonzero association IDs; an ID is not reused until close
  or expiry has completed within the session generation.
- Address family, address/domain encoding, port, inner/outer lengths,
  association identity, and datagram hard limit must all pass before the relay
  creates, selects, resolves, sends on, or otherwise uses a UDP socket.
- DNS associations receive scheduling priority only. They do not receive a
  distinct wire privilege or bypass resource accounting.

### Close, errors, flow control, and privacy

- The only current v1 message types are generated constants for
  `UDP_DATAGRAM(0x10)`, `UDP_ERROR(0x11)`, `PING(0x20)`, `PONG(0x21)`,
  `CLOSE_ASSOCIATION(0x30)`, and `CLOSE_SESSION(0x31)` with the directions in
  `.spec/relay-protocol.md`.
- Relay exit, framing failure, SSH lane-A loss, cancellation, or channel reset
  terminates the session generation and all owned associations exactly once.
- Backpressure is end-to-end: the Apple pump stops reading new HEV datagrams
  when its bounded outbound queue has no credit; Go uses bounded queues and one
  stdout writer so frames cannot interleave. Saturation drops datagrams with
  counters and bounded semantic errors; it never retries without a bound.
- Default diagnostics contain only stable local error codes, state transitions,
  aggregate counts, limits, redacted family, and build identity. No payload,
  domain, destination, remote-controlled error string, or command stdin is
  logged.

## Candidate binding to evaluate after foundation acceptance

The following is a recommended concrete shape, not an adopted decision. It is
intentionally explicit so the resumed architect can accept, rename, or reject
each slot against the foundation ADR rather than rediscovering the design.

| Ownership | Candidate artifact | Owner and rule |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Authoritative schema | `Protocol/Relay/relay-v1.schema.json` | Handwritten, source-controlled, language-neutral JSON. Owns numeric values, widths, order, direction, reserved ranges, errors, and named hard/negotiable limits. It does not describe sockets or runtime policy. |
| Build-only generator | `scripts/relay-protocol-tool.py` | Handwritten Python-standard-library tool; no runtime dependency. Validates schema and emits both bindings in canonical order. Pin its behavior with fixtures and source review. |
| Swift generated output | `Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/RelayProtocol/Generated/RelayProtocolV1+Generated.swift` | Checked-in generated source; values/metadata only. Header names schema hash and regeneration command. No manual edits. |
| Go generated output | `relay/internal/protocol/generated_v1.go` | Checked-in generated source; values/metadata only. `gofmt` is part of generation. No manual edits. |
| Swift handwritten layer | `Sources/ReluxTunnelCore/RelayProtocol/` excluding `Generated/` | Byte cursor/writer, incremental decoder, hello/session state, typed errors, and `SSHExecChannel` adapter. Must not import NetworkExtension or a concrete SSH engine. |
| Go handwritten layer | `relay/internal/protocol/` excluding `generated_v1.go` | Incremental decode/encode and session state over bounded `io.Reader`/`io.Writer`; no socket calls. |
| Go UDP ownership | `relay/internal/udp/` | Association registry, resolver/socket use, queues, timers, DNS scheduling. Consumes only validated protocol values. |
| Canonical vectors | `Protocol/Relay/Vectors/v1/*.json` | Source-controlled synthetic cases with stable IDs, hex bytes, chunk plans, semantic result/error, and limits. Generated/audited independently of both production codecs. |

Recommended deterministic commands, subject to the foundation ADR:

```sh
make relay-protocol-generate
make relay-protocol-check
```

`relay-protocol-generate` runs the pinned build-only tool from the repository
root, writes both outputs in a fixed order, applies `gofmt` and Swift formatting,
and rejects an unclean schema. `relay-protocol-check` regenerates into a
task-scoped temporary directory, byte-compares both checked outputs, runs schema
negative fixtures, compiles the Swift and Go bindings, and fails stale,
hand-edited, or nondeterministic output. Generated diffs are committed and
reviewed together with the schema; reviewers never approve a schema-only change.

### Candidate byte and stream primitives

- Swift: bounds-checked integer shifts over `Data`/byte collections; no
  alignment-dependent `withUnsafeBytes.load`, no FFI, no blocking syscall, no
  `readToEnd`, and no buffer sized from an unchecked peer length. The async
  frame pump reads through `SSHExecChannel.read(maximumBytes:)` and honors
  partial `writeSome` acceptance plus the channel-policy caps.
- Go: `encoding/binary.BigEndian`, `io.ReadFull` only after validating the fixed
  prefix, fixed-capacity buffers/pools, and one bounded reader goroutine plus
  one bounded writer goroutine around stdin/stdout. Blocking OS stdio calls are
  confined to those goroutines; no unbounded channel/map, `io.ReadAll`, cgo,
  `unsafe`, reflection, or runtime schema parsing.
- Both: explicit checked conversion between wire unsigned widths and local
  integer types; typed errors preserve session-versus-association disposition;
  cancellation/EOF releases retained buffers and generation-owned state.

